          "text": "CUDA graphs allow you to capture a sequence of kernel launches and their dependencies, then replay the entire sequence as a single unit. The primary speedup comes from **eliminating per-launch CPU overhead**. In eager execution, each kernel launch requires the CPU to issue a command to the GPU via the driver, which involves validation, argument marshaling, and synchronization checks. For thousands of small kernels, this CPU-side overhead can dominate, leaving the GPU idle waiting for the next command. A graph replay bypasses this by submitting the entire pre-validated dependency DAG to the GPU in one go, so the GPU can execute kernels back-to-back with minimal CPU involvement. This is especially beneficial for workloads with many tiny kernels, where the launch latency (often 5\u201310 microseconds per kernel) is comparable to or exceeds the kernel execution time itself.\n\nThe second major benefit is **explicit dependency optimization**. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CUDA runtime must infer dependencies from stream semantics and memory operations, often inserting implicit synchronizations or conservative barriers. With a graph, you explicitly declare which kernels depend on which, allowing the GPU to overlap independent kernels across streams more aggressively and to skip unnecessary global syncs. The graph also lets the driver pre-allocate and reuse memory buffers",
